<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Devon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Devon is a county of south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, lying between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a> with coasts on both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> Channel. Its name comes from the Dumnonii, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> people of the peninsula, through the post-Roman kingdom of Dumnonia. The area was absorbed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> in the 7th and 8th centuries, and its county town is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exeter">Exeter</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/devon.jpg" alt="Devon" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Neil Theasby · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dartmoor_ponies_by_the_B3212_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4546147.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Devon is a ceremonial county in South West England, bordered by the Bristol Channel to the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a> to the east, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> to the south and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> to the west. It straddles a peninsula and so, uniquely among English counties, has two separate coastlines. It contains <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dartmoor">Dartmoor</a> and part of Exmoor, two upland moors that are the source of most of its rivers, including the Taw, Dart and Exe. The longest river, the Tamar, forms most of the border with Cornwall. The southeast coast is part of the Jurassic Coast World Heritage Site, and the county gives its name to the Devonian geologic period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human remains from 30 to 40,000 years ago have come from Kents Cavern in Torquay, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dartmoor">Dartmoor</a> is thought to have been occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> hunter-gatherers from about 6000 BCE. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> and Roman periods the region belonged to the Dumnonii, a Celtic British people whose name is thought to mean deep valley dwellers, and from which the name Devon derives. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> held the area under military occupation for around 350 years. After the formal Roman withdrawal in 410 CE, one of the leading Dumnonii families attempted to create a dynasty and rule as kings of Dumnonia.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> incursions from the east began around 600 CE, first as small bands settling along the coast of Lyme Bay and the southern estuaries. Devon became a frontier between the Britons and Anglo-Saxon Wessex, and Dumnonia was partly assimilated into the kingdom of Wessex. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a> set the western boundary with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> at the Tamar in 936. William Camden, in his Britannia of 1607, described Devon as one part of an older, wider country that once included Cornwall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The county town is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exeter">Exeter</a>. Plymouth, the largest settlement and a historic port, and the Torbay conurbation of Torquay, Paignton and Brixham are administratively separate. Many of the resorts on the coast grew rapidly with the arrival of the railways in the 19th century. Devon is known for its mariners, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Sir Francis Drake</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-raleigh">Sir Walter Raleigh</a> and Robert Falcon Scott. The cream <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> of scones, jam and clotted cream is thought to have originated in the county, though neighbouring counties also claim it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Devon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wessex was a farming kingdom of chalk downland, fertile river valleys and, in the west, the heavier lands of Somerset and <b>Devon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Other parts of the island kept Celtic languages: Gaelic, and perhaps some Pictish, in most of Scotland, Cornish in Cornwall and parts of <b>Devon</b>, Cumbric perhaps to the 12th century in parts of Cumbria, and Welsh in Wales.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-english">Old English · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Tin from Cornwall and <b>Devon</b> was used to make bronze from about 2200 BCE, and Britain became the first region of Europe to switch all its metalwork to tin-bronze, in 2200–2100 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/bell-beaker-culture">Bell Beaker culture · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Lyte was a curate in County Wexford from 1815 to 1818, where he often preached at the church in Killurin and came under the influence of its rector, the Reverend Abraham Swanne, which stayed with him; he was later vicar of All Saints' Church in Brixham, <b>Devon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/abide-with-me">Abide with Me</a></li><li><blockquote>Migrants from Dumnonia in south-west Britain, the land of modern <b>Devon</b> and Cornwall, settled the northern coast of Armorica in the 5th and 6th centuries and gave it the name of their homeland.</blockquote><a href="/state/domnonee">Domnonée</a></li><li><blockquote>The name survived in the post-Roman Kingdom of Dumnonia and in the county name <b>Devon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/dumnonii">Dumnonii</a></li></ul></section>
